entry.summary:
The Problem
You're working on a 20,000-line JSONL (JSON Lines) dataset with carefully curated training data. You make changes, but need to verify what actually changed between versions. The lines are too long. They don't fit on your screen. Each line is a dense unformatted JSON. You reach for your favorite diff tool. And it fails. Or worse—it shows you a meaningless blob of changes because it's treating your entire JSONL file as a single JSON document. This shouldn't happen. But it does, constantly, to engineers and data engineers everywhere. JSONL (JSON Lines) is deceptively simple: one valid JSON object per line. {"id":1,"name":"Tom","age":35} {"id":2,"name":"Maria","age":32} {"id":3,"name":"Alex","age":28}
It's not the same as pretty-printed JSON with newlines. Each line is completel…
